## Findings
1. advisory — brief:33 The promise is that a failed background read "changes only the read-failure notice", and Interfaces brief:65 restates it. In the prototype, the only visible `.screen-notice` for a non-empty queue sits in the reading pane's action bar. Below 840px that pane is `display: none` until a row is opened, so at 390 a Patron looking at the list after a background failure sees the stale queue with no visible failure signal. Only the visually hidden `.shell__status` region announces it. Served b6 at 390 opens the seventh item before aborting, so it never sees this. Writes stay safe, because the server refuses a stale id. Replacement for brief:33's clause: "it changes only the read-failure notice, shown above the list pane in every layout (and repeated in the action bar when detail is open)", and add to b6 (brief:131): "at 390 with detail closed, the failure notice is visible above the list". [severity: S2] [class: spec×promise-unattainable-under-accepted-domain] — check: none: a served W-407-b6 step at 390 with the list showing (detail closed) that aborts one interval read and asserts a visible `Could not load the inbox.`
2. advisory — brief:65 says a removed selected id "retains its last reading snapshot and reason" until another row is selected. When that ordinary refresh empties the queue (the Patron's only petitio answered elsewhere), `InboxView` returns early with `Nothing waiting on you`. The detached reading, withdrawn notice and half-typed draft then disappear from screen, though they remain in state. Replacement: append to brief:65 "when the removal leaves the queue empty, the empty line replaces the retained reading; the draft is kept in state but not shown". Alternatively, render the detached decision ahead of the empty early return. [severity: S2] [class: spec×promise-unattainable-under-accepted-domain] — check: none: a served b6 step that removes the sole selected petitio and asserts the chosen behaviour
3. advisory — brief:65 says "a stale in-flight ordinary read cannot consume a later answer reconciliation". That holds, but there is a second effect. If the ordinary read was already in flight and resolves after the write landed, it settles with `intent === null` and no answered id. `keepDetached` then shows the just-answered item as `This petitio no longer needs a decision.` with disabled controls, until the queued intent read advances. It is a transient false withdrawal shown next to the success confirmation. Replacement: append to brief:65 "while an explicit answer is queued, an ordinary settle that lacks the answered id does not detach it" (for example, skip `keepDetached` when `refreshIntentRef.current !== null`), or state it as a limit. [severity: S3] [class: tests×ruled-case-uncovered] — check: none

